 ## Overview
-Normal Alignment Regularization is a geometric regularization technique used in differentiable volume rendering and 3D Gaussian Splatting to align the learned scene representation with the underlying physical surface. It solves the problem of misaligned Gaussians or density fields by penalizing deviations between rendered normals and external geometric constraints, such as planar surfaces, depth-derived normals, or prior normal maps [S1, S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from sibling methods by focusing on general surface alignment via external constraints rather than specific alignment to ray directions [4202-normal-ray-alignment-loss.md] or ground-truth normal labels [4232-normal-ground-truth-alignment-loss.md].
